#{[17592186589107 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\\},$ and the set $S=\\{0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\\}$ has $|S|=9$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}, & x=0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ there are $5$ items $(3,4,5,6,7)$ that satisfy $2<X\\le 7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(2<X\\le 7)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}=\\frac{5}{9}.\n$$"] [17592186589129 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{5,9,12,13,14,15\\},$ and the set $S=\\{5,9,12,13,14,15\\}$ has $|S|=6$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}, & x=5,9,12,13,14,15 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ there are $2$ items $(5,9)$ that satisfy $X<12.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<12)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186589979 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{2}],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1) & =P(1\\le X\\le \\frac{9}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9/2}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2}-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186589981 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $V$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(v)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le v\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $V$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{1}{2},2],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(v)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}, & -\\frac{1}{2}\\le v\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1\\le V<\\frac{1}{2}) & =P(-\\frac{1}{2}\\le V<\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}f(v)\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\frac{5}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186589993 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[k,11k],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10k},\\, & k\\le x\\le 11k, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X<6k) & =P(k<X<6k) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{k}^{6k}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{k}^{6k}\\frac{1}{10k}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6k-k}{10k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590003 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $W$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le w\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $W$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[3,11],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8},\\, & 3\\le w\\le 11, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W\\ge 7) & =P(7\\le W\\le 11) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{7}^{11}f(w)\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{7}^{11}\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{11-7}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590006 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[0,5],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(0\\le X\\le 3) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3-0}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590010 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-7,3],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10},\\, & -7\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-5<X\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{-5}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{1}\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-5)}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590012 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[0,12],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 12, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1\\le X\\le 3) & =P(0\\le X\\le 3) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{12}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3-0}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590027 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-h,5h],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6h},\\, & -h\\le x\\le 5h, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 3h) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3h}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3h}\\frac{1}{6h}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3h-0}{6h} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590035 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-5,-2],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3},\\, & -5\\le x\\le -2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-4) & =P(-5\\le X<-4) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-(-5)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590040 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{7}{3}],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & \\frac{1}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{7}{3}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & =P(1<X\\le \\frac{7}{3}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{7/3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{7/3}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{3}-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590050 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $V$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(v)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le v\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $V$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-2,0],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(v)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & -2\\le v\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V\\le -\\frac{1}{2}) & =P(-2\\le V\\le -\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1/2}f(v)\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}-(-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590061 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $U$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le u\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $U$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & -\\frac{5}{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U\\ge 0) & =P(0\\le U\\le \\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}f(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2}-0)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590078 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-4,0],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & -4\\le x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-\\sqrt{2}) & =P(-4\\le X<-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-\\sqrt{2}}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}-(-4)}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590755 "An exponential random variable $Y$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nY∼\\text{Exp}(\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSo, $Y$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\pi {e}^{-\\pi y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(1\\le Y\\le 3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\pi {e}^{-\\pi y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\pi y}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\  & ≈0.0431\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590757 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-x/6}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X>3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-x/6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/6}|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590769 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2x/3}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X<2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2x/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x/3}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4/3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.7364\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590785 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x/3}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X>11)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>11) & ={\\int }_{11}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{11}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/3}{|}_{11}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-11/3} \\\\  & ≈0.0256\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590792 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(0.8).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.8{e}^{-0.8x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(2<X\\le 6)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 6) & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}0.8{e}^{-0.8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.8x}{|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4.8}+{e}^{-1.6} \\\\  & ≈0.1937\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590794 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(4).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(\\frac{1}{2}<X<\\frac{3}{4})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{2}<X<\\frac{3}{4}) & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{3/4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{3/4}4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}{|}_{1/2}^{3/4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3}+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.0855\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590802 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(5).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-5x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(\\frac{2}{5}<X\\le 1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{2}{5}<X\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{2/5}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2/5}^{1}5{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5x}{|}_{2/5}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5}+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1286\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590803 "An exponential random variable $W$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda w}, & w\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nW∼\\text{Exp}(0.25).\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.25{e}^{-0.25w}, & w\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(1\\le W\\le 5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le W\\le 5) & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f(w)\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}0.25{e}^{-0.25w}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.25w}{|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1.25}+{e}^{-0.25} \\\\  & ≈0.4923\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590806 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X<1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.9502\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590816 "An exponential random variable $Y$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nY∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $Y$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4y/3}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(Y\\ge 2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4y/3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-4y/3}|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-8/3} \\\\  & ≈0.0695\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590819 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x/3}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X\\le 2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-x/3}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.4866\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590834 "An exponential random variable $Y$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nY∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $Y$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2y/5}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(Y\\le 4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 4) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2y/5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2y/5}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-8/5}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.7981\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590838 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X>2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-6} \\\\  & ≈0.0025\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186591622 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/2c/2cc3b27a1f926e9f5b5fdf59a5ea01fb7e28da53c7fc01535cc590f4c315258c.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{y}^{1}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{y}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=y}^{x=1} \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}+3x{y}^{2}]}_{x=y}^{x=1} \\\\  & =[({1}^{3}+3\\cdot 1\\cdot {y}^{2})-({y}^{3}+3\\cdot y\\cdot {y}^{2})] \\\\  & =1+3{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1+3{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186591627 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/cf/cf0c8438253a5b46a468610ca62bc17a9ce927c98045eb461291d98644241e2f.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $-1\\le y\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}\\frac{3x}{2({y}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2({y}^{2}+1)}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2({y}^{2}+1)}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4({y}^{2}+1)}[1-{({y}^{2})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4({y}^{2}+1)}[1-{y}^{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1-{y}^{2})(1+{y}^{2})}{4({y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1-{y}^{2})}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(1-{y}^{2})}{4}, & -1\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186591633 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $y\\ge 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{3}+3y)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-3{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{3}+3y)}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\infty } \\\\  & =-3{[{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{3}+3y)}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\infty } \\\\  & =-3[0-{e}^{-(0+3y)}] \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3y}, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186591641 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{6}\\,{x}^{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}[{2}^{4}-{0}^{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{y}^{1/3}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{2}}{[\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{4/3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}}[{1}^{4/3}-{0}^{4/3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186591656 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot y-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{f}_{Y}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}(\\frac{1}{2}+u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{u}{2}+\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[y+{y}^{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{y+{y}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{Y}(y)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ \\frac{y+{y}^{2}}{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(y)=\\frac{y+{y}^{2}}{2}.$"] [17592186591663 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3<Y<1) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186591671 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\pi }, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186591672 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(x+y)}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-{e}^{-y}]}_{y=0}^{y=b} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }(1-{e}^{-b}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $x\\ge 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-u}]}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ 1-{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)=1-{e}^{-x}.$"] [17592186591684 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a3aeba14383b64a0414f3163828d972102073dc972d64105e779b317b8574b0.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{16}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{[\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{[x{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}[x\\cdot (\\sqrt{x})^{4}-x\\cdot (\\sqrt{0})^{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}[x\\cdot {x}^{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{64}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186591687 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}+1\\cdot {y}^{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{f}_{Y}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{u}{2}+\\frac{{u}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[y+{y}^{3}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{y+{y}^{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{Y}(y)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ \\frac{y+{y}^{3}}{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(y)=\\frac{y+{y}^{3}}{2}.$"] [17592186591701 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+x{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}+(2){y}^{3}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(4+2{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}y+\\frac{1}{32}{y}^{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(2)+\\frac{1}{32}{2}^{4}]-[\\frac{1}{4}(1)+\\frac{1}{32}{1}^{4}] \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186591702 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+y)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y), & 0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186591710 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(x+y)}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-(x+y)}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty } \\\\  & =(0+{e}^{-(x+0)}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186591713 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{10}{3}x{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}{y}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{y}^{4}({2}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =5{y}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<\\