\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $y\\le 0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{-y}}2x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{-y}}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{y}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{-y}} \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}{e}^{y}]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{-y}} \\\\  & =[{(\\sqrt{-y})}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{y}-{0}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{y}] \\\\  & =-y{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-y{e}^{y}, & y\\le 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186593211 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.92),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.92, & x=1 \\\\ 0.08, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=0.08.\n$$"] [17592186593225 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"] [17592186593231 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.1),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.1, & x=1 \\\\ 0.9, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=0.9.\n$$"] [17592186593242 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186593265 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.77),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.77, & x=1 \\\\ 0.23, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=0.77.\n$$"] [17592186593270 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.16),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.16, & x=1 \\\\ 0.84, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=0.16.\n$$"] [17592186593293 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.27),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.27, & x=1 \\\\ 0.73, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=0.73.\n$$"] [17592186593296 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.87),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.87, & x=1 \\\\ 0.13, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=0.13.\n$$"] [17592186593304 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186594147 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x+y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2+2y-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(y+1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(y+1)}{3}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 2,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{x+y}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2(y+1)}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{2(y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{2(y+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<\\frac{2}{3}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2/3}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2/3}\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}{2(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2/3}(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[{x}^{2}+x]}_{0}^{2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{2}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\left(\\frac{10}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186594155 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-2x-y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{-y}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{-y}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}\\right){|}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }(1-{e}^{-2b}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}, & \\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2x-y}}{{e}^{-y}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x}, & \\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186594161 "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen sticker. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{7,\\,12,\\,28,\\,35,\\,78,\\,81,\\,99,\\,112\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X>70).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{7,\\,12,\\,28,\\,35,\\,78,\\,81,\\,99,\\,112\\},$ and the set $S=\\{7,\\,12,\\,28,\\,35,\\,78,\\,81,\\,99,\\,112\\}$ has $|S|=8$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}, & x=7,\\,12,\\,28,\\,35,\\,78,\\,81,\\,99,\\,112 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X>70$ are $78,\\,81,\\,99,\\,112.$ There are $4$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>70)=4\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{4}{8}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186594166 "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen marble. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{7,8,…,22\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(10\\le X<17).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{7,8,…,22\\},$ and the set $S=\\{7,8,…,22\\}$ has $|S|=22-7+1=16$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}, & x=7,8,…,22 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $10\\le X<17$ are $10,11,12,13,14,15,16.$ There are $7$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(10\\le X<17)=7\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}=\\frac{7}{16}.\n$$"] [17592186594172 "Let $X$ represent the card number Ann picks. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{15,16,…,40\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(20\\le X<25).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{15,16,…,40\\},$ and the set $S=\\{15,16,…,40\\}$ has $|S|=40-15+1=26$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{26}, & x=15,16,…,40 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $20\\le X<25$ are $20,21,22,23,24.$ There are $5$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(20\\le X<25)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}=\\frac{5}{26}.\n$$"] [17592186594182 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}\\frac{3x}{8}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{8}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{8}\\cdot (x+2-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3x(-{x}^{2}+x+2)}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(-{x}^{2}+x+2)}{8}, & \\,0\\le x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x<2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3x}{8}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3x(-{x}^{2}+x+2)}{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-{x}^{2}+x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{-{x}^{2}+x+2}, & \\,0\\le x<2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186594191 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{xy+1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}+x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [2y+2-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2(y+1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(y+1)}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{xy+1}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2(y+1)}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy+1}{2(y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy+1}{2(y+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186594193 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nFrom the equation above, it follows that\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x<1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{15{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{2})}{2} \\\\  & =15{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ equals $0.$ As a result, the product will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the joint probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}15{x}^{2}y, & 0\\le x<1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186594214 "Let $X$ represent the selected answer. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{1,2,…,5\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\le 2).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{1,2,…,5\\},$ and the set $S=\\{1,2,…,5\\}$ has $|S|=5-1+1=5$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}, & x=1,2,…,5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\le 2$ are $1,2.$ There are $2$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 2)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"] [17592186594218 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y}^{1}\\frac{3(x+2y)}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{y}^{1}(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2xy]}_{x=y}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+2y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\frac{1+4y-5{y}^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+4y-5{y}^{2})}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(1+4y-5{y}^{2})}{4}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le y\\le x\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3(x+2y)}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3(1+4y-5{y}^{2})}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2y)}{1+4y-5{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x+2y)}{1+4y-5{y}^{2}}, & \\,0\\le y\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<\\frac{2}{5}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{5}) & ={\\int }_{y}^{2/5}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{1}{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/5}^{2/5}\\frac{2(x+2\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right))}{(1+4\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)-5{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/5}^{2/5}\\frac{2(x+\\frac{2}{5})}{\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1/5}^{2/5}(5x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+2x]}_{1/5}^{2/5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186594229 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}2x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x{[{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2x(2-1) \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x{e}^{y}}{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<Y\\,|\\,X=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)) & =P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)<Y\\,|\\,X=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3/2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right))\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3/2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{e}^{y}]}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3/2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186594234 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\frac{21{x}^{2}y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{21{x}^{2}}{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{21{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{{x}^{2}}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{21{x}^{2}}{4}\\cdot (1-{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{4})}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{4})}{4}, & \\,-1<x\\le 0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $-1<x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{21{x}^{2}y}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{21{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{4})}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{4}}, & \\,-1<x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186594235 "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen LEGO block. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{1,2,…,14\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(8\\le X\\le 14).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{1,2,…,14\\},$ and the set $S$ has $|S|=14$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{14}, & x=1,2,…,14 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $8\\le X\\le 14$ are $8,9,10,11,12,13,14.$ There are $7$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(8\\le X\\le 14)=7\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186594238 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{9({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{16}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3({y}^{2}+3)}{16}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{{y}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{{y}^{2}+3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186594725 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/01/012c298cc2defbf82e240f266f94733cdcd8e579ad10535eb94f33becf528b8d.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not completely cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186595919 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,3]\\times [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{9}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{9}x[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{9}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{9}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186595922 "The marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2y}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{(x+1)}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{b+1}) \\\\  & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot 2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186595923 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y{e}^{-2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,\\infty )\\times [1,3].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y{e}^{-2x}, & \\,0\\le x,\\,1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3{e}^{-2x}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{e}^{-2}}{8}-\\left(-\\frac{3}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186595936 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(x+1)\\sqrt{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,2]\\times [0,1].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}(x+1)\\sqrt{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}(x+1)\\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3(x+1)}{8}{[\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{x}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186595940 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{32}{e}^{y}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,2]\\times [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡9].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{32}{e}^{y}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1,0\\le Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡9) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}\\frac{3}{32}{e}^{y}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{32}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}[{e}^{y}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186595958 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $2$ and $3$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{2,3\\}.$"] [17592186595966 "The marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x{y}^{2}+2}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2x]}_{x=2}^{x=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8{y}^{2}+8-2{y}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(6{y}^{2}+4) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot ({y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186595989 "The marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot y-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+y, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{y}{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186595993 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1)y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1)y, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186595996 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot 4{y}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [1,2]\\times [0,1].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186596002 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe indicator function of the set $\\{1,2\\}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/4c/4ccd0aab0a3e67bfed1fe90f4868b374fb9e443da559aa3cc34b4033c7e40427.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $1$ and $2$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$"] [17592186596663 "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system I since $3$ and $6$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(3,6)=3>1\n$$\n\n- The theorem applies to system II. The numbers $9$ and $16$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $9\\cdot 16=144.$\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system III since $6$ and $24$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(6,24)=6>1\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""]}
